        "type": "list",
        "items": [
          "Court jurisdiction: Pursuant to Clause 5, Article 26, Point a, Clause 1, Article 35, and Article 39 of the CPC, inheritance disputes fall under the jurisdiction of the Regional People’s Court, determined territorially as follows: (a) where the estate comprises immovable property, jurisdiction lies with the Court where the immovable property is located; (b) where the estate consists solely of movable property, jurisdiction lies with the Court where the respondent resides or works. [5]",
          "Pre-action mediation requirements for land use rights: Under Clause 2, Article 3 of Resolution No. 04/2017/NQ-HĐTP of the Council of Justices of the Supreme People’s Court and the 2024 Land Law, unlike disputes determining who holds land use rights, disputes over the division of inherited land use rights are not subject to mandatory mediation at the commune-level People’s Committee prior to filing a lawsuit. Accordingly, the claimant may file directly with the competent Court without a local failed-mediation record. [5]",
          "Required filings when the dissenting heir withholds original title documents: The family must prepare the decedent's Death Certificate, documents proving blood or marital relationships, the will (if any), and family meeting minutes or evidence of having served a division proposal. If the dissenting heir holds the original Certificate of Land Use Rights and refuses to surrender it, the claimants may contact the Land Registration Office to obtain cadastral archive extracts or petition the Court to collect evidence pursuant to Clause 2, Article 106 of the CPC; simultaneously, they may request the Court to apply provisional urgent measures, such as prohibiting the transfer of property rights under Article 121 of the CPC, if there is a risk of the dissenting heir unilaterally dissipating the assets. [5]"
        ]
